1
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:07,549
In the kingdom of Pañchāla, King Drupada kindled a great sacrificial fire —

2
00:00:07,549 --> 00:00:13,325
and out of the flames arose a daughter. Draupadī: dark, radiant, born of no womb.

3
00:00:13,325 --> 00:00:17,390
The gods whispered that she would bend the fate of kings.

4
00:00:19,790 --> 00:00:24,184
When she came of age, kings from every land filled Drupada's hall.

5
00:00:24,184 --> 00:00:27,579
High above hung a golden fish, spinning on a wheel.

6
00:00:27,579 --> 00:00:34,635
Whoever could string the great bow, and pierce the fish's eye by its reflection alone, would win her hand.

7
00:00:37,035 --> 00:00:40,641
One by one, the proud kings strained against the bow —

8
00:00:40,641 --> 00:00:43,313
and one by one they fell back, defeated.

9
00:00:43,313 --> 00:00:47,921
Even Karṇa the golden rose to try; but Draupadī turned her face away.

10
00:00:50,121 --> 00:00:54,466
Then, from among the brahmins, a quiet young man in white stood up.

11
00:00:54,466 --> 00:01:01,404
The five Pāṇḍava brothers, believed dead in the burning of the lac house, had come to Pañchāla in disguise.

12
00:01:01,404 --> 00:01:02,701
His name was Arjuna.

13
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:09,795
He circled the bow, and bowed his head. In a single breath he strung it.

14
00:01:09,795 --> 00:01:14,817
Then, gazing only into the water — at the trembling reflection of the wheel —

15
00:01:14,817 --> 00:01:17,099
he loosed five arrows into the sky.

16
00:01:21,299 --> 00:01:22,696
The golden fish fell.

17
00:01:22,696 --> 00:01:28,483
And Draupadī came to the stranger, and laid her garland of white flowers upon his neck.

18
00:01:28,483 --> 00:01:33,073
In the crowd, Kṛṣṇa smiled — for he alone had recognized the brahmin.

19
00:01:35,673 --> 00:01:40,780
That evening, at their mother's door, the brothers called out: "Mother —

20
00:01:40,780 --> 00:01:42,554
see what we have brought!

21
00:01:42,554 --> 00:01:47,306
" And Kuntī, without turning, answered: "Share it among yourselves.

22
00:01:47,306 --> 00:01:50,995
" A mother's word, once spoken, could not be unsaid.

23
00:01:53,195 --> 00:02:01,711
But the sage Vyāsa revealed a deeper design: in a former life, Draupadī had prayed five times to Lord Śiva for a worthy husband —

24
00:02:01,711 --> 00:02:04,220
and Śiva had granted all five prayers.

25
00:02:04,220 --> 00:02:11,416
And so Draupadī wed the five Pāṇḍava brothers; and their fates were bound together, until the end of the age.
